### 3-Week Implementation Plan (2 Developers)

| Week | Developer 1 (Backend & Integration) | Developer 2 (DevOps & Frontend) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Week 1** | • Write `mpesa_bunker.php` script.<br>• Design database schema & unique constraints.<br>• Write `mpesa:process-bunker` daemon. | • Set up local testing environment simulating Safaricom callbacks.<br>• Configure Supervisor on staging/production.<br>• Write unit tests for duplicate payments. |
| **Week 2** | • Implement Daraja Transaction Status Query API.<br>• Build the "Claim Missing Payment" backend logic. | • Set up Deployer for zero-downtime deployments.<br>• Configure Nginx routing for the bunker script.<br>• Build the "Claim Payment" UI for parents/admins. |
| **Week 3** | • Build Admin Dashboard to view "Unallocated Payments" (flagged payments).<br>• End-to-end integration testing. | • Load testing (simulating 100 concurrent callbacks during a deploy).<br>• Production deployment and monitoring. |

---

### Verification of Constraints

1. **Two developers, three weeks:** The tasks are highly modular. Developer 1 focuses on PHP/Laravel logic, Developer 2 focuses on Nginx, Supervisor, Deployer, and UI.
2. **KES 0 additional spend:** 
   * Supervisor is free.
   * Deployer is free.
   * Writing to disk uses existing SSD storage (JSON files are < 1KB; 10,000 transactions = 10MB).
   * No new servers or paid SaaS are introduced.
3. **15-Minute SLA:** The Supervisor daemon processes files within 5 seconds of receipt. If a callback is lost entirely, the "Claim Payment" portal resolves it in under 60 seconds.
4. **Never credited twice:** Guaranteed by the MySQL `unique` index on `mpesa_transaction_id` combined with database transactions. Even if two processes attempt to write the same transaction simultaneously, MySQL will reject the second write.
